<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle (1748)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1748">1748</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Peace of Aix-la-Chapelle (1748) · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle, signed in October 1748, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a>. Most conquests were returned: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> gave back the Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisbourg">Louisbourg</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>'s succession was recognised but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> kept Silesia. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, Parma, Piacenza and Guastalla went to Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> kept further districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-aix-la-chapelle-1748.jpg" alt="Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle (1748)" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacques Dumont le Romain · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques_Dumont_-_All%C3%A9gorie_en_l%27honneur_de_la_publication_de_la_paix_d%27Aix-la-Chapelle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> opened talks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a> in 1746. The Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a>, who controlled British foreign policy, delayed them in hopes of a better position, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> army collapsed and British hopes of recovering the Austrian Netherlands failed. France, though victorious in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, was hard pressed by the British naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>, and after the Second Battle of Cape Finisterre in October 1747 could no longer protect its merchant convoys. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> had made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and was kept in the war only by British subsidies. A congress assembled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> on 24 April 1748, and a draft was agreed on 30 April.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The final treaty was signed on 18 October 1748 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> acceded in documents of 4 December 1748 and 21 January 1749. All accepted the Pragmatic Sanction of 1713, and Austria recognised the Prussian acquisition of Silesia. Austria ceded Parma, Piacenza and Guastalla to Philip of Spain and minor territories, including Vigevano, to Sardinia, and withdrew from Modena and Genoa.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> withdrew from the Austrian Netherlands and returned the Dutch Barrier forts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen-op-zoom">Bergen op Zoom</a>. Britain and France exchanged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisbourg">Louisbourg</a> on Île-Royale for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>. Spain renewed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asiento">Asiento</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negros">Negros</a>, the monopoly contract to supply slaves to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>, which Britain had held since 1713; Britain renounced it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-madrid-1750">Treaty of Madrid</a> in 1750 for £100,000. France agreed to expel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edward-stuart">Charles Edward Stuart</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The treaty largely failed to settle the issues that had caused the war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, which doubled in size and wealth with Silesia, gained most. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> resented her exclusion from the talks and the pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to cede territory, while many in Britain questioned the value of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> paid to her. These tensions led to the Diplomatic Revolution and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>, which began in 1756. In the Treaty of Aranjuez of 1752 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> agreed to respect each other's boundaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the return of conquests, forced by the state of its finances, gave rise to the phrase as stupid as the Peace. The treaty ended the First Carnatic War between the British and French in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Celebrations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> featured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-frideric-handel">George Frideric Handel</a>'s Music for the Royal Fireworks.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle (1748)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After he became king of Naples in 1735, Parma passed to the Habsburgs, and the <b>Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle</b> of 1748 then gave Parma, Piacenza and Guastalla to Philip of Bourbon, founding the Bourbon-Parma line.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
